WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.940
An architecture comparison needs the complete path and a common requirement.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.480
Compare where conversion occurs, which segments carry high current, and what each

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.840
equipment input requires.

4
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.880
Then include protection, thermal integration, service isolation, footprint and retrofit constraints.

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.100
A design can reduce one distribution burden while adding conversion or service

6
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.140
complexity elsewhere.

7
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.800
A retrofit may be constrained by existing equipment and facility interfaces; a

8
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.600
new deployment may have different options.

9
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.560
Neither context makes a voltage or topology universally best.

10
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.540
Use a short tradeoff matrix rather than a single efficiency headline.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.700
Define the operating points and conditions behind any numerical comparison.

12
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.820
The twenty twenty-six converter discussions help identify tradeoffs, but their topology examples

13
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.820
are not a substitute for qualification of the selected assembly.

14
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.880
For our case, the winning proposal must address the limiting segment and

15
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.280
preserve the required operating and fault behavior across the rest of the

16
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:55.640
system.

